+# SenseNova-Loop-9B small-Mac quant candidates
+
+Candidate specs for the 16 GB and 8 GB Mac tiers, published before the Metal measurements finish so a CUDA sweep can
+run in parallel. The Mac owns memory, speed and the final Metal gate; numbers land in
+`docs/recipes/sensenova-loop-9b-metal.md` when measured.
+
+Source: `sensenova/SenseNova-Loop-9B` @ `329a65f701ba8c48b73c30d0cbaa6f0449733673`, BF16. Format: MLX affine
+(u32 bitstream codes, bf16 scales and biases), the same format the Metal loader reads per module.
+
+## Converter
+
+`tools/sensenova_loop_mlx.py` on this branch:
+
+```bash
+python3 tools/sensenova_loop_mlx.py --source SenseNova-Loop-9B --out OUT \
+  --recipe docs/small-quant-recipes/RECIPE.json --clip-search 10
+```
+
+- `--recipe`: top-level `bits`/`group` are the global width; the first `rules[].match` regex that matches the
+  engine-side module path (`language_model.model.layers.N.mlp.down_proj`, `language_model.model.embed_tokens`,
+  `language_model.lm_head`) sets that tensor's `bits`/`group`. Overrides are written as MLX per-module
+  `quantization` entries in `config.json`.
+- `--clip-search N`: per group, N shrink steps of the min/max range from 1.0 to 0.5, keeping the step with the least
+  squared error after bf16 rounding of scale and bias. 0 (default) reproduces the previous min/max quantizer.
+- 3, 5 and 6-bit codes are packed as MLX's continuous little-endian bitstream (codes cross u32 edges); 2, 4 and
+  8-bit output is unchanged. Earlier commits packed 3/5/6-bit wrongly; use this branch.
+- MTP (`mtp.`), vision (`model.visual.`) and `loop_gate` tensors are skipped. The same script converts stock
+  `Qwen/Qwen3.5-9B` for the control.
+
+All groups are 64. The mHC routers (f32), norms, `A_log`, `dt_bias` and `conv1d` stay unquantized.
+
+## Tiers
+
+| recipe | tier | global | lm_head | embed | self_attn q/k/v/o | linear_attn in_proj_a/b | other linear_attn | MLP | weights |
+|-|-|-|-|-|-|-|-|-|-|
+| `q4` (no recipe, no search) | 16 GB | 4 | 4 | 4 | 4 | 4 | 4 | 4 | 4.70 GiB |
+| `s16.json` | 16 GB, higher quality | 4 | 6 | 6 | 6 | 8 | 4 | 4 | 5.29 GiB |
+| `s8-b.json` | 8 GB | 2 | 3 | 2 | 4 | 8 | 3 (in_proj_qkv, out_proj); 2 (in_proj_z) | 2 | 2.99 GiB |
+| `s8-d.json` | 8 GB, smallest | 2 | 3 | 2 | 3 | 8 | 2 | 2 | 2.80 GiB |
+
+`s8-*` and `s16` are converted with `--clip-search 10`; `q4` is the existing checkpoint (min/max, no search).
+
+## Budgets these target (measured on Metal, simulated with `TF_QWEN27_WORKING_SET_BYTES`)
+
+- 16 GB Mac: about 10.5 GB at 8k context. The current q4 peaks at 7.60 / 7.82 / 9.13 GiB at 4k / 8k / 32k,
+  `--parallel 1`, 512 prompt rows.
+- 8 GB Mac: about 5.0 GB (4.66 GiB) at 4k. Non-weight overhead at 4k with 128 prompt rows is about 1.85 GiB, so the
+  weights must be at most about 2.8 GiB. Both `s8` recipes sit at that edge; quality decides whether either ships.
+
+## Quality gate to compare against
+
+Teacher-forced top-1 agreement over 64 positions on the three gate prompts (`p0-plain`, `p1-code`, `p2-reason`)
+against the BF16 reference continuation. Metal q4 against BF16: plain 57/64, code 62/64, reasoning 61/64. Control:
+stock Qwen3.5-9B through the same converter and recipe against its own BF16 continuation.
